### DG-6.0 slice — guest scaffold certification-contract ratchet

**Audit finding:** Harbor's missing `operatorCapabilities` was not an isolated
manifest omission. `workspace:create --guest` also omitted the block, so every
new G1 scaffold would fail `guard:workspace-certification` after registry
generation.

**Ratchet:** guest scaffolds now emit all three current operator capabilities as
explicit `false` values. The scaffold test asserts the exact fail-closed shape,
and the guest-scaffold ADR / enforcement guide document the contract.

**Status DG-6.0 (2026-07-31):** generator gap closed without enabling any
operator feature or changing a workspace certification tier.

### DG-6.1 slice — product HTTP host base contract

**Stem audit:** the recorded Urban↔Denali overlap remains 16 basenames, but most
rows are thin compatibility adapters or intentionally different W-domain
services. The remaining exact structural clone was the common request-context,
response, error, and workspace-type portion of both product host-port types.

**P-contract:** `WorkspaceProductHttpHostBasePorts` now owns those five shared
host contracts in `workspace-sdk/http`. Urban and Denali extend that base while
keeping their store, exposure, registration, settings, booking, destination,
and reminder dependencies workspace-owned.

**Status DG-6.1 (2026-07-31):** host-port shape unification completed without
moving handlers into the API host or weakening product-specific port types.

### DG-6.2 slice — cross-workspace clone ratchet

**Gap:** package-local clone-budget specs protected newly generated HTTP trees,
and `guard:workspace-peer-import` blocked direct package imports, but no central
guard prevented an existing G1 workspace from accumulating Denali-parallel
product basenames.

**Guard:** `guard:denali-gravity-clones` scans `http`, `auth`, `tours`, `catalog`,
and `exposure`. Harbor and Guest Club may overlap Denali only on four neutral
loader/compose filenames (`index`, `react`, `routes`, `routes-manifest`). Urban's
audited 16-stem proof baseline may shrink but cannot grow.

The guard is included in `verify:product`; it measures structural regression,
not raw LOC, and does not force deletion of legitimate thin adapters.

**Status DG-6.2 (2026-07-31):** full repository clone ratchet added.

### DG-6.3 slice — remove workspace HTTP method compatibility mirrors

**Audit:** Urban and Denali each retained a one-line private
`workspace-http-method.ts` re-export. Neither file was part of the package export
map; each only fed its local route manifest. Harbor and Guest Club already use
the SDK type directly.

**Cleanup:** both route manifests now import `WorkspaceHttpMethod` from the SDK,
and the two private mirrors are deleted. The Urban↔Denali exact-stem count drops
from 16 to 15. The clone-guard baseline is lowered in the same slice so the
deleted stem cannot return.

**Status DG-6.3 (2026-07-31):** redundant compatibility mirrors removed.

### DG-6.4 slice — lower the clone baseline after cleanup

**Audit correction:** DG-6.3 initially allowed the measured overlap to shrink
while retaining a 16-stem allowlist. That would have permitted the deleted
`workspace-http-method.ts` mirror to return without failing the guard.

The audited Urban allowlist and reported ceiling are now both 15. Future cleanup
must lower the allowlist in the same change; any reintroduced or new parallel
stem fails immediately.

**Status DG-6.4 (2026-07-31):** ratchet is strict at the post-cleanup baseline.

### DG-6.5 slice — reproducible G1 adapter-budget metric

**Audit:** Harbor's adapter surface is measured from the governed HTTP, auth,
tours, catalog, and exposure paths, while the charter's ≤15 target excludes
neutral barrels/loaders. The executable metric prevents that claim from
drifting as the workspace grows.

The clone guard counts TypeScript files in those governed paths while excluding
the neutral `index.ts`, `react.ts`, and `routes.ts` basenames. It retains
`routes-manifest.ts` as workspace configuration. Harbor currently measures
**12/15** and the guard fails if the adapter surface grows past 15.

Harbor reached the recorded G1 smoke on the scaffold date (2026-07-31); this is
a same-day execution record, not yet a multi-workspace lead-time benchmark.

**Status DG-6.5 (2026-07-31):** adapter budget is executable and time-to-G1 has
an explicitly scoped baseline; the current Harbor result is **12/15**.

### DG-6.6 slice — executable workspace-role labels

**Audit correction:** the TEMP baseline grouped all `finance-ws*` packages as
fixtures. Existing manifests are more precise: finance-ws2/3/4/6 are
`supported: false` + `registryOnly: true`; finance-ws5 is a supported finance
consumer proof, and booking-ws2 is a supported booking proof. The latter two
still are not certified guest product families.

`guard:denali-gravity-workspace-roles` now enforces those existing roles and
